/*****************************************************************************
    jitDegradeT -- the fixture for the LOUD-degrade crossover primitive.

    jitDegrade is the ONLY worked example of docs/jit.md S0's crossover policy
    ("degrade to the oracle LOUDLY"), and until this file it had no test -- the
    two call sites are reached only when a jitted walk meets an ITERATOR, and no
    fixture drove that combination. An instrument with no test is exactly the
    "a green stub reads as coverage" failure the speller pin exists to catch.

    `testing(<coded action>)` routes to jitRunAction, which sets jitting=1 and
    executes the action's BlocK. `++` on an iterator then reaches opPlusPlus's
    isIterator arm under jitting, which is the degrade site.

    EXPECTED: one "=== JIT DEGRADE #n ..." line per advance, ON STDERR, naming
    the construct and the node -- AND the loop still produces the right answer,
    because degrade means run-it-interpreted, not refuse.

    ⚠⚠ THIS FIXTURE DOES NOT CURRENTLY REACH THE DEGRADE SITE, AND IT IS
    COMMITTED IN THAT STATE ON PURPOSE. Read this before "fixing" it.

    RESULT AS OF 2026-07-30: exit 0, sentinel present (so nothing truncated --
    see the parse-failure trap in CLAUDE.md), the interpreted walk correct, and
    ZERO degrade lines. `testing(walkBag)` routes to `jitRunIfTest`, not
    `jitRunAction`:

        extern GroupItem testing(GroupItem input)      (Commands.rtn)
            if input.isCoded    return jitRunAction(input) ...
            return jitRunIfTest(input) ...

    So `walkBag` does not arrive `isCoded`. The mechanism is NOT broken --
    `incant/jitscratch` reaches `jitRunAction` 22 times in the same build. The
    difference is WHERE THE ACTION IS DEFINED: `jitAdd` and friends live in
    `incant/generate`, an INCLUDED file, and arrive coded; `walkBag` is defined
    in this scratch file's own `define` block and does not. Same shape
    otherwise (`name code={ ... };`, no argument -- the argument form was tried
    first and behaves the same).

    ⇒ THE OPEN QUESTION, and it is not this fixture's job to answer: why does a
      `define` in an included file yield an isCoded field when the identical
      define in a top-level script file does not? Plausibly the same family as
      bear-trap #15 (bare top-level script statements behave differently from
      the same code inside a registered action), but NOT established.

    ⇒ CONSEQUENCE WORTH STATING PLAINLY: `jitDegrade`'s two call sites are
      currently UNREACHABLE BY ANY FIXTURE IN THE TREE. The crossover policy's
      only worked example has no behavioural test -- which is precisely why it
      was about to be deleted with its host without anyone noticing. The lift
      preserved the pattern; it did NOT give it coverage, and claiming
      otherwise would be the "a green stub reads as coverage" failure.

    TO FINISH THIS: get a coded action carrying an iterator into `jitRunAction`
    -- either by resolving the isCoded difference above, or by defining the
    fixture action in an included file the way `incant/generate` does.
*****************************************************************************/
